---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import Markdown from '../components/Markdown.astro';
import Tombstone from '../components/Tombstone.astro';
import { path } from '../lib/paths';
import { LEIDEN, LICENCE, SITE } from '../lib/site';

/** Demonstrates the markdown-and-TeX pipeline with a definition every reader already
 *  knows, so the example carries no claim of its own. */
const mathExample = String.raw`The Euler product for $\zeta(s) = \sum_{n \ge 1} n^{-s}$:

$$\zeta(s) = \prod_{p \text{ prime}} \frac{1}{1 - p^{-s}}, \qquad \Re(s) > 1.$$`;
---

<Page
  title="About"
  description="What MathemAct is for, why accounts follow a fixed schema, and how verification works."
  lead="MathemAct is a record of how mathematicians actually use AI tools, kept in a form that can be read as evidence rather than as opinion."
>
  <h2>What this is for</h2>

  <p>
    Something is happening to mathematical practice, and almost none of it is written
    down. Tools get used in the middle of real work, they help or they mislead, and the
    account of what happened stays in a corridor conversation or a group chat. When the
    paper appears there is often no trace of any of it.
  </p>

  <p>
    That is a poor position for a discipline to be in while it decides its norms. Norms
    set on the basis of impressions tend to be either too permissive or too strict, and
    both are expensive. MathemAct exists to give the argument something to stand on.
  </p>

  <h2>Principles and practice</h2>

  <p>
    In {LEIDEN.published} the <a href={LEIDEN.url}>{LEIDEN.name}</a>, endorsed by the
    International Mathematical Union, asked researchers to <q>{LEIDEN.quotes.disclosure}</q
    >. It added, sensibly, that <q>{LEIDEN.quotes.evolving}</q>.
  </p>

  <p>
    MathemAct is the layer underneath. It is not a general forum about AI and it takes no
    position on whether these tools are good for mathematics. Its job is narrower: to
    accumulate a corpus concrete and well-structured enough that a journal could adopt the
    posting schema as a disclosure template, and that anyone arguing about norms has cases
    to point at.
  </p>

  <p class="disclaimer">
    MathemAct is an independent project. It is not affiliated with the Leiden Declaration,
    its working group, or the International Mathematical Union, and carries no endorsement
    from any of them.
  </p>

  <h2>Why a fixed schema</h2>

  <p>
    Every constraint the form places on what you can write is deliberate. A free-text box
    produces prose that reads well and cannot be compared with anything. A schema produces
    accounts that can be filtered by task, grouped by tool, counted by outcome, and read
    side by side five years from now. This is closer to a reporting standard — CONSORT,
    PRISMA — than to a message board, and it is meant to be.
  </p>

  <p>
    One field carries more weight than the rest. <strong
      >How you verified correctness</strong
    > is required on every account, with no skip and no <q>not applicable</q>. An account
    without it records that something felt right, which is not a finding. With it, the
    account tells a reader what standard of checking was actually applied, and that is the
    thing a mathematician needs in order to judge whether to trust the rest.
  </p>

  <p>
    Accounts that report failure are worth as much as accounts that report success, and
    often more, because failure modes are what nobody publishes. Nothing in the form or
    the interface treats a failure as the lesser contribution.
  </p>

  <h3>Writing mathematics</h3>

  <p>
    Accounts are written in Markdown, and TeX between dollar signs is rendered when the
    page is built. Nothing is sent to a third-party service to typeset it.
  </p>

  {/* Wrapped rather than styled via a class on <Markdown> directly: Astro's scoped
      styles key off a per-file attribute that is not applied to a child component's root
      element, so `.example` on the component would compile to a selector that matches
      nothing. The wrapper is an element of this file, so it is scoped normally. */}
  <div class="example">
    <Markdown content={mathExample} />
  </div>

  <h2>How verification works</h2>

  <p>
    Three signals, each independent. Institutional and ORCID stack on top of Registered.
  </p>

  <dl class="tiers">
    <dt>Registered</dt>
    <dd>
      A confirmed email address at any domain. Shows your display name and nothing else.
    </dd>

    <dt>Institutional</dt>
    <dd>
      A confirmed email address whose domain matches a record in the
      <a href="https://ror.org/">Research Organization Registry</a>. Shows the institution
      name, the country, and the month the check was made.
    </dd>

    <dt>ORCID-linked</dt>
    <dd>
      An ORCID iD resolved against the public ORCID API. Shows an icon linking to your
      record.
    </dd>
  </dl>

  <p>
    Affiliation is derived from your confirmed email address by the database itself, never
    from anything you type. There is no field anywhere that lets you set your own
    institution, and the columns holding affiliation and verification dates cannot be
    changed by the account that owns them.
  </p>

  <p>
    Domain matching uses the registry's own domain records and the longest matching
    suffix, so <code>maths.ox.ac.uk</code> resolves to Oxford. It does not use rules of
    thumb about <code>.edu</code> or <code>.ac.uk</code>, because those fail on exactly the
    institutions this site is for — <code>ens.fr</code>, <code>inria.fr</code>,
    <code>mis.mpg.de</code>, <code>sissa.it</code>, <code>weizmann.ac.il</code>.
  </p>

  <h3>What a badge does and does not claim</h3>

  <p>
    A badge states only what was checked: that on a particular date, an email address at a
    particular institution's domain was confirmed to work. It does not claim you are
    faculty there, that you hold any particular position, or that you still work there
    today. The date of the check is always shown, and re-verification is prompted once a
    year.
  </p>

  <p>
    Your email address itself is never displayed, never returned by the API, never included
    in the export, and never shown to moderators.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>A pseudonym is allowed at every tier</strong>, including with an institutional
    badge. Admitting to heavy reliance on these tools still carries professional risk, and
    an account that can only be given under a real name is an account that will not be
    given. Do not attempt to work out who a pseudonymous contributor is.
  </p>

  <h2>How a practice ages</h2>

  <p>
    Model, version, and date of use are required on every account, and any signed-in
    mathematician can record that a practice still works or no longer does. Listings sort
    by recency. A practice written against a 2025 model may be actively misleading by
    2026, and the interface has to say so without being read closely:
  </p>

  <ul class="legend" role="list">
    <li>
      <Tombstone status="verified" /> — a correctness check is on record and someone has confirmed
      it still works
    </li>
    <li><Tombstone status="unverified" /> — nobody has confirmed it either way</li>
    <li><Tombstone status="stale" /> — not rechecked since an older model</li>
    <li><Tombstone status="broken" /> — someone tried it and reported that it failed</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Moderation, licence, and your data</h2>

  <p>
    Moderation is volunteer-run, which means it is careful and slow rather than instant.
    Anything posted can be reported, and anything reported can be hidden while it is
    looked at. The <a href={path('/code-of-conduct/')}>code of conduct</a> sets out what is
    expected and what happens when it is not met.
  </p>

  <p>
    Contributed content is published under <a href={LICENCE.url} rel="license"
      >{LICENCE.fullName} ({LICENCE.name})</a
    >. You keep copyright in what you write and you are credited. Everything published is
    exported nightly as JSON, so the corpus can be analysed or archived by anyone without
    scraping the site.
  </p>

  <p>
    Deleting an account genuinely removes your profile and detaches your content rather
    than merely hiding it. What is stored, who processes it, and how to have it erased are
    set out in the <a href={path('/privacy/')}>privacy notice</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Who runs this</h2>

  <p>
    MathemAct was conceived by Anna Zanoli, a PhD researcher at the Institute of Science
    and Technology Austria (ISTA). The website is built and maintained by her sister Emma.
    The code is <a href={SITE.repository}>public</a>, and questions, corrections, and
    objections are welcome at
    <a href={`mailto:${SITE.contactEmail}`}>{SITE.contactEmail}</a>.
  </p>

  <p>Posting opens once accounts and moderation are in place.</p>
</Page>

<style>
  .disclaimer {
    padding-inline-start: var(--space-sm);
    border-inline-start: var(--border-width-thick) solid var(--rule);
    color: var(--ink-muted);
    font-size: var(--size-2);
  }

  /* The rendered TeX sample. Set on a raised surface so it reads as a specimen of the
     rendering rather than as part of the argument. */
  .example {
    padding: var(--space-md);
    border: var(--border);
    background-color: var(--surface);
  }

  .tiers {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .tiers dt {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-md);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--size-1);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wide);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-muted);
  }

  .tiers dd {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-3xs);
    margin-inline-start: 0;
  }

  .legend {
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  /* Inline flow, not flex — see the note in index.astro. A flex li turns the trailing
     description into a single unbreakable item that wraps to its own line. */
  .legend > li + li {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-2xs);
  }
</style>
